Latest from Claude:

Here are the remaining checks from the list, in the order I’d run them:

Powers and integers. BEGIN { print 2^31, 2**53, 123456789, 1e10 }. This also exercises the %.30g change from earlier. If you haven’t applied it yet, 123456789 will still print as 123456788.99999....
Number formatting. BEGIN { print 0.1, 0.1+0.2, 3.14159265, 1/3 } and BEGIN { printf "%d %5.2f %e\n", 42, 3.14159, 12345.678 }. Compare with your host awk.
Operators that use the fixed characters. BEGIN { print (1 != 2), ("a" !~ /b/), (1 || 0) }.
Regex ranges. /[a-z]/, /[A-Z]/ and /[0-9]/ against lines mixing letters, digits and punctuation. This is the one I’d test most carefully, since EBCDIC letters have gaps between i/j and r/s and b.c may expand ranges numerically.
Options and files. -F:, -v x=5 and -f progfile.
Stubs. BEGIN { "date" | getline x } and print "x" | "cat" should give your clean error, not an abend.
